  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">National Appeal &quot;Samen in Actie voor Beiroet&quot;</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Due to the impact of the blast, tough Covid-19 contention measures, severe political turmoil and financial and economic downturn, hundreds of thousands of Lebanese still find themselves in a desperate situation, with many unmet needs such as access to:
&#x2612; drinking water due to lack of electricity (fuel for generators is scarce)
&#x2612; opportunities to gain own income in order to be able to purchase basic necessities (food, NFI, housing, hygiene, water), and pay for basic social services (health, education, communication)
&#x2612; protection services (SGBV, unsafe &amp; unfair labour conditions)</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 4 August, at around 6:00pm, a warehouse at the Beirut Port containing large quantities of
ammonium nitrate exploded. After an initial explosion, a subsequent blast caused widespread
damage, with reports of damage reaching more than 20 km from the port area. The explosions and
ensuing fires reportedly released toxic materials in the environment. Official governmental reports
estimate that 214 people were killed, over 8,000 injured, and more than 300,000 people left homeless in the Greater Beirut area/Mount Lebanon. 
Tens of buildings in the port area including grain silos storing around 85 percent of the country&#x2019;s grain, and numerous residential places have also been damaged or destroyed, including many healthcare facilities and several major hospitals in Greater Beirut area, due to the blast.</narrative>
  </description>
